Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||06-25-2000||01:36 AM||||I've heard people talk about greasing the "sliders" on the calipers, is this an 84-87 thing? or is it for the '88s too? I ask because while I was tearing everything apart for the engine drop, it didn't appear as if anything slid, there were 2 torx head bolts holding the caliper to the spindle, and 2 19mm bolts holding the inside and outside of the callipers together, to tell the truth, just by looking att it as I was tering it apart, I couldn't tell how the outside brake pad even tightened down onto the rotor. Any idea if this set up is correct or if somone did an incorrect rebuild?||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||06-25-2000||08:31 AM||||The torx bolts that hold the caliper onto the spindle are the slider pins. All Fieros and most American disc calipers with one piston use slider bolts. Push those torx bolts completely out. There are two rubber o-rings that the slider rides on inside of each of the caliper bolt holes. Those should be replaced too and the bolt holes cleaned out. The slider has a metal machined sleeve that moves a little bit back and forth. Make sure this is not stuck, clean it up good, then grease it up the best you can with a synthetic high temp brake grease.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||06-25-2000||09:31 AM||||But don't "brake" anything!  haha  (Very poor attempt at humor.)||||reg||1
